Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Surface intersection: the parts of an open line beyond its vertices
(mitcad#263).

GeomInt_LineConstructor splits an analytic line of two surfaces (a line,
parabola or hyperbola) at its vertices and keeps the parts between them
that lie in both surfaces' domains. A line with no vertex is kept whole;
with two or more, the parts before the first vertex and after the last were
dropped although they lie in both domains (on unbounded surfaces always).
A cone and a plane parallel to its axis meet in a hyperbola whose asymptotes
are the cone's generatrices parallel to the plane: where the cone's seam is
one of them within rounding (a chamfer's cone with its seam 8.6e-9 rad off
the direction of a wall square to it), the hyperbola crosses the seam twice
some 1e8 mm away, and of the branch through the wall only a piece of no
length between those two vertices was left. The chamfer then found no cut
of its cone by the wall and failed (it went on to a walking that chamfers
do not implement).

- GeomInt_LineConstructor::Perform, for such open lines with vertices: the
  part from the line's start to its first vertex and from its last vertex
  to its end are tested at a point inside them (one unit from the vertex on
  an unbounded side) as the parts between vertices are, and kept when in
  both domains, each as a part of its own. The parts are not joined where
  they meet (mitcad#1879): a vertex between two parts may be where the line
  crosses a periodic surface's seam, and the boolean operations need the
  line split there. Joined, the parts between vertices that the line
  constructor had always kept apart came out as one curve across a cone's
  seam, and OCCT's own cases boolean/volumemaker C5 C6 E4 E6 F7 F8 G2 (a
  cone and planes parallel to its axis, cut into cells by mkvolume) lost
  cells. Lines whose outer parts lie outside a domain now get exactly the
  parts they got without this patch.

Upstream: new.

Test: geometry/tests/test_chamfer_overflow.cpp,
test_chamfer_of_a_post_beyond_a_square_plate (ctest
geometry.suite.chamfer_overflow): a post's foot chamfered past a square
plate's outline, the post's seam at several angles and the plate turned by
1e-8 (its sides then nearly parallel to the seam): valid, the volume of the
chamfer's ring less its parts beyond the sides (the turned plate fails
without this patch by the test's documentation). Another caller:
geometry/tests/test_fillet_corner_cases.cpp,
test_rounding_end_prolonged_across_a_seam (ctest
geometry.fillet_corner_cases), whose volumes move by 1e-6 mm3 when the
intersection is split. It fails without the patch (shown 2026-11-20 by
tools/occt-port-proof.py).

